    {
      "ts": 1790896443.707747,
      "status": "done",
      "result": {
        "summary": "The compact echo and the warn-rows notice look right, and the three acceptance tests are present. I did not run any tests. Two problems remain in the new `ids` path of `bus.read`: an unvalidated id reaches a file path, and the id-order and skipped-notice criteria are not met or not tested.",
        "verdict": "request_changes",
        "comments": [
          {
            "path": "orchestrator/bus.py",
            "line": 200,
            "issue": "Caller-supplied ids go straight to get(), which builds TASKS / f\"{tid}.json\" without validation. An id such as '../../some/file' reads any readable .json file outside the tasks dir. With full=True the whole parsed object is returned into Planner context. Previously read() only touched ids from the index. Validate ids against the task-id pattern (e.g. ^T-\\d+$) and count non-matches as skipped. A malformed or non-dict json file would also raise an uncaught JSONDecodeError or TypeError instead of being skipped.",
            "severity": "med"
          },
          {
            "path": "orchestrator/bus.py",
            "line": 200,
            "issue": "Acceptance says ids returns the tasks 'in id order'. The code keeps the caller's order and does not dedupe, so ids=[b, a, a] returns [b, a, a]. The new test passes ids already in ascending order, so it cannot catch this. Use sorted(set(ids)) when building rows.",
            "severity": "med"
          },
          {
            "path": "tests/test_bus.py",
            "line": 445,
            "issue": "Acceptance requires the unknown id 'T-9999' to be reported in the skipped notice. The test never captures stderr, so it does not assert 'skipped 1 unreadable rows'. It also uses an id from the other parent where the criteria name two ids a and b. Add a stderr assertion (contextlib.redirect_stderr).",
            "severity": "low"
          },
          {
            "path": "orchestrator/bus_mcp.py",
            "line": 47,
            "issue": "read_warn_rows is used unvalidated. A non-int value in pool.toml, such as a string, makes `len(rows) > warn_rows` raise TypeError outside the try block. Coerce it to int with a fallback of 200. The try block also does not cover a non-dict 'bus' value (AttributeError is caught there, fine).",
            "severity": "low"
          }
        ],
        "packet_version": "6cacc0a3b6d8",
        "confidence": 0.0,
        "provenance": [
          "repo"
        ]
      }
